  "description": "A cross-platform .NET library that downloads and maintains a local store of LLM models. Pulls models from the Ollama registry with sha256 verification and resumable downloads, keeps them in Ollama's own on-disk blob and manifest layout, lists, shows, copies, deletes and creates models from Modelfiles, reads GGUF metadata, and resolves a model name to the GGUF file on disk for an in-process runner such as CodeBrix.Ollama.ModelRunner. Also obtains models that no such registry serves - the files of a Hugging Face repository, of any list of HTTPS addresses or of a folder on disk - into the same store, and materializes them as the publisher's own file tree. Exports a model to ONNX - passing through the graphs a publisher already ships, or running the ONNX Runtime GenAI model builder or Hugging Face Optimum in a CPython the machine already has - and keeps the result in the same store as a derived bundle with its provenance. Reduces an exported graph - dynamic INT8, or block-wise four- or eight-bit weights - into another derived bundle a few times smaller than the one it came from; the weight-only modes, and dynamic INT8 on a prepared graph, are done by the library's own managed ONNX codec and quantizer, so making an existing graph smaller needs nothing installed and writes the same bytes ONNX Runtime's own tools write. Converts a llama-family checkpoint - safetensors or PyTorch containers, one file or several shards, with a GPT-2 byte-level or SentencePiece tokenizer - into an ordinary GGUF model in the same store, which ResolveAsync then hands to an in-process runner; that conversion is the library's own managed code as well, so it needs nothing installed and no Python at all. Stores a quantized copy of a GGUF model beside it, under a name that says which quantization it is, with the source's template, parameters and licence carried over and the provenance recorded - the quantizer itself is a delegate the application supplies, because this library depends on no inference engine. Pure managed code; no server, no native libraries. It has exactly one NuGet dependency, CodeBrix.Python, which stays inert until a Python feature is used: obtaining, listing, resolving and materializing models never load it, and neither does the GGUF conversion or the managed reduction engine.",
